### Expected behaviour

The UPF should handle excessive QER references gracefully—either by rejecting the PFCP request with an appropriate cause code or by safely expanding/limiting the pool—rather than terminating the entire process.

### Observed Behaviour

The UPF immediately aborts during session installation: the fifth PDR causes ogs_pfcp_qer_find_or_add() to request another QER from the session’s pool, the pool cannot supply one, the internal assertion fires, and open5gs-upfd terminates. All existing user-plane sessions are dropped, resulting in a remote DoS.

