### Expected behaviour

During PFCP rule installation (e.g., Session Establishment Request handling), the UPF should validate FAR.DestinationInterface against supported capabilities:

If DestinationInterface=2 is not supported, the UPF should reject the FAR / reject the session establishment with an appropriate PFCP Cause, rather than accepting a configuration that will later crash the data plane.

Alternatively, if DestinationInterface=2 is intended to be supported, the UPF should implement the corresponding forwarding path so that matching user-plane packets are handled correctly.

In the data plane, encountering an unsupported DestinationInterface should result in dropping the packet and logging an error, not triggering FATAL + abort().

### Observed Behaviour

The UPF accepts PFCP association/session establishment and successfully installs a FAR with DestinationInterface=2.

When a GTP-U packet subsequently matches the corresponding PDR, the UPF logs a fatal “not implemented” message and aborts:

FATAL: Not implemented : FAR-DST_IF[2] (../src/upf/gtp-path.c:707)

FATAL: _gtpv1_u_recv_cb: should not be reached. (../src/upf/gtp-path.c:708)

The open5gs-upfd process terminates (Aborted (core dumped)), dropping all user-plane traffic until the service is restarted.

